a former shale gas fracking site in  little plumpton in lancashire the  community here dismayed that the  government is due to lift a ban on  drilling  chris and susan holliday live close by  and have fought fracking every step of  the way well it does feel like they've  done this on a whim we were that close  to  the site being closed down and restored  and now it seems to  everything's up for grabs again  i'm astounded because the moratorium was  put in place in 2019 because of the  earthquakes the government have always  said that it'll stay in place unless the  science changes as far as i'm aware  there's nothing being  published to say that the science has  changed sections of a leaked government  commissioned report from the british  geological survey appear to say just  that  but the same report also points out that  other industries are allowed to cause  more seismic activity than fracking ever  was and suggests more consistent  standards  it's what fracking companies have been  lobbying for  if you apply the regulations that you  apply to shale gas to the construction  geothermal quarrying or coal mining  industry none of them would be able to  operate in the uk  looser regulations are what activists  like nick danby fear we're in the grip  of a climate crisis and to even be  considering launching a brand new fossil  fuel industry is just absolutely absurd  it makes no sense whatsoever given that  this government is is committed to net  zero by 2050.  the new prime minister disagrees  this is about much more than just  fracking for gas here in lancashire this  trust has said her government is  preparing to grant around 100 new  licenses for oil and gas drilling in the  north sea in essence she is ushering in  a new era of fossil fuel exploration and  extraction here in the uk  it's good news for an industry already  enjoying record profits  although the government says cheap  renewables are also part of the answer  critics say the uk is going backwards  searching for more oil and gas when it  should be doing more to stop using them  all together hannah thomas peter sky  news
 

